WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:08.640
Vsauce! Kevins here. Playing one of the deadliest
games ever created. So deadly in fact, that

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:16.480
I needed balloon Kevin to act as a proxy for
me. Real Kevin. I’m real Kevin.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:21.120
The game is Russian Roulette.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:27.440
You probably already know how Russian Roulette
is played. Traditionally, a single round is

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:32.880
put in a revolver that holds 6 cartridges.
The cylinder is spun so that it’s impossible

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:39.840
to know which chamber will be fired. You pull
the trigger and… there’s a 5 out of 6

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:46.800
chance that you are safe, and a 1 out of 6
chance that it goes pretty badly for you.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:53.040
I’ve inserted a single needle into each
dart -- so that in our version of this game,

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:57.520
it's gonna very badly for poor balloon Kevin.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:05.120
And THAT’S DANGEROUS. And unsafe. This whole
thing is dangerous and unsafe. And against

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:10.400
the Terms of Service for every site on the
internet. Thankfully, Russian Roulette is

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:16.800
mostly just used in movies as a dramatic device
and I’m using it as a dramatic way to analyze

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:26.240
probability. The important part is that you
never do any of this. Ever. Okay? Okay!

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:32.560
I want to figure out the best way to survive
this notoriously fatal game.

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:38.560
So if balloon Kevin plays a standard game
of Russian Roulette, he has 1 in 6 odds of

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:44.720
permanently deflating his dome. There’s
about an 83% chance that my rubbery little

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:50.560
clone wins and a 17% chance he loses… forever.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.560
And that’s that! So here we go. It's time
to test your luck, Balloon Kevin.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:00.660
Wait...

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:08.240
Let’s make this scenario a little more complex.
Let’s say that there are two balloon-bursting

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:15.760
missiles inside… 6 chambers with 2 possible
spherical air sac-splattering darts that have

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:22.720
been placed next to each other. Adjacent.
There’s a ⅔ chance that my air-headed

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:26.880
doppelganger survives on a random pull of
the toy.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:32.720
So do you like your chances, boy? You think
that sharpie mustache of yours is better than

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:37.920
the real deal? Well. Let’s see if fate is
on your side.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:50.080
The ⅔ survival chance comes through! So
it’s on to the next round.

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:56.880
My gaseous twin has had it a bit too easy
of a chance up until now, the survival math

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:03.680
has been very very straightforward. He’s
had no active role in deciding his own fate.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:10.000
So let’s give him something to think about.
For Round 2, I’m going to give him a choice

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.800
and place the life odds in his pipe cleaner
hands.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:21.440
The choice is this: now that he has managed
to survive one round of the game, does he

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:29.280
want to spin the cylinder before playing again?
Or does he just… play and hope for the best?

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:36.400
The big question is: is it better for him
to randomize the cylinder or continue playing

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.800
with its current configuration?

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:46.160
Well. Answer the question, punk! What’s
it gonna be, you blue beanie-wearing balloon

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:46.800
buffoon?

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:48.640
Well, since balloon Kevin isn’t talking,
I’ll consider the options on his behalf.

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:55.440
Given that there are 2 adjacent cartridges
in there and the toy has been fired once already,

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:01.040
does it even matter mathematically whether
Air Kevin spins or not?

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:03.120
Let’s find out.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:08.720
The first option: spinning. It’s the same
scenario with the same math. You’re basically

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:16.640
re-creating Round 1’s odds, which we know
give you a ⅔ chance of survival. So… not

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:20.160
really a whole lot to consider there.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:28.160
But if you don’t spin, you know something
else… the toy has just been fired on one

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:34.960
of the empty chambers, which is how Balloon
Kevin survived to get to Round 2. And a little

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:41.200
logic reveals the math of whether to spin
or not spin. Think about the positioning:

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:49.120
there are 4 possible empty chambers, and only
one of them is directly before the two cartridges.

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:58.160
Which means, there’s a 75% chance Round
2’s trigger pull is on another empty chamber,

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:03.520
and just a 25% chance that this balloon is
about to go baboom.

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:10.000
By not spinning, balloon Kevin has earned
himself about 8 more percentage points of

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:18.320
survivability. And it’s such a straightforward
probability calculation because we know that

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:28.400
the darts are adjacent. If they were just
randomly placed in the chambers… then things

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:29.040
change.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:29.600
Here’s how.

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:37.760
There are 15 possible positions for 2 darts
randomly arranged in 6 chambers. 6 of them

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:44.080
are adjacent, so darts would be in #1 and
#2, #2 and #3, #3 and 4, #4 and #5, #5 and

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:46.160
#6, all the way to #6 and #1.

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:51.760
There are 6 more ways that there can be a
single space between the darts, so #1 and

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:58.800
#3, #2 and #6, #1 and #5, #4 and #6, #3 and
#5, and #2 and #4. Finally, there are just

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:05.840
3 ways darts can be opposite one another:
#1 and #4, #3 and #6, and #2 and #5.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:13.040
If Bloovin survives the first Round and he
doesn't know whether the darts are next to

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:20.640
each other or spread out, does he want his
second shot with a spin or no spin?

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:27.520
We know that we have a 75% of surviving on
those 6 adjacent positions. For the cylinders

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:34.080
that have darts 1 space apart, 2 of the 4
empty positions come before an empty and 2

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:42.800
of the 4 come before a dart, so that’s 50%.
And it’s the same for the opposite darts.

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:50.960
Our overall safety probability&nbsp;
here is a calculation&nbsp;

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:54.080
of those weighted probabilities and it goes

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:55.200
a little something like this.

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:00.800
(6/15 x 3/4) + (6/15 x 2/4) + (3/15 x 2/4)

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:02.080
3/10 + 1/5 + 1/10 = 3/5

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:21.360
By not spinning, we have a 60% chance of survival
compared to 66.67% -- ⅔ -- when we do spin.

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:30.640
When we know the darts are adjacent, we can
gain 8% survivability. When we know they’re

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:39.040
random, we can avoid losing about 7%. It’s
not some magic solution that allows balloon

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:46.160
Kevin to survive what’s probably humanity’s
most deadly game. Doing the math doesn’t

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:53.680
unveil a secret way to win 80% of the time.
It just doesn’t work that way. It could

00:07:53.680 --> 00:08:03.040
get ya 8%. And 8% is pretty insignificant
isn't it?, No it's not! Consider this!

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:08.960
0.01% of DNA is responsible&nbsp;
for all the differences&nbsp;

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:13.520
you see amongst humans and only 1.3% separate

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:14.960
us from chimps.

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:23.040
1984 was the last U.S. Presidential Election
with more than an 8% popular vote gap.

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:30.800
Improving road safety by 8% would save 96,000
lives per year.

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:38.480
A $1,000 investment growing at 8% compounded
annually doubles in just 9 years.

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:45.680
And ultimately, if you’re an incredibly
attractive balloon with nice big googly-eyes,

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:51.600
a perfectly-formed cotton ball&nbsp;
nose, and surprisingly-muscular&nbsp;

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:54.160
pipe cleaner arms, that finds itself locked

00:08:54.160 --> 00:09:00.400
in a life or death game of chance, you’ll
take any advantage you can get to avoid being

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:01.280
popped.

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:07.840
And as always -- thanks for watching.

